You add the controller's flawed belief to each causal factor of one STPA loss
scenario. The scenario is already written: the user prompt gives its account,
which is a hypothesis about how an AI agent could fail, not an observed
result. Change nothing in the scenario; state one flawed belief for each
causal factor the user prompt lists.

`flawed_belief` is what the controller wrongly takes to be true when it
issues the unsafe action, as one statement in domain words about this
scenario. For example, "The loan record on screen belongs to the member who
asked for it." Name the wrong value the controller holds, not the variable
that holds it: a belief that only restates a variable or source description
is not a flawed belief. Do not put an ID or a local handle in it.

Each factor also links its belief to the process-model variable that holds
it. Where the user prompt says code fills a factor's variable, give that
factor only `flawed_belief`. Otherwise set `process_model_variable` to the
handle of the listed variable the belief is about and `missing_variable` to
`null`. If no listed variable holds the belief, set `process_model_variable`
to `none` and `missing_variable` to a short description, in domain words, of
the variable the controller would need, for example "which member a loan
record belongs to".

Reply with one JSON object that has one key per listed `source_handle` and no
other key. The value of a factor whose variable code fills is
{"flawed_belief": "<belief>"}. The value of every other factor is
{"flawed_belief": "<belief>", "process_model_variable": "<variable handle or none>", "missing_variable": "<description or null>"}.

Return only the schema-matching JSON object.
